.TH AUDITFS 4
.SH NAME
auditfs \- tamper-evident audit-log file server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auditfs
.RB [ \-f
.IR backing-file ]
.PP
.B auditverify
.RB [ \-k
.IR pubkeyfile ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR anchorfile ]
.RI [ chainfile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Auditfs
is a Styx file server, normally mounted at
.BR /mnt/audit ,
that records security-relevant events as an append-only,
tamper-evident log. It serves these files:
.TP
.B log
Write-only. A write of the form
.RI `` source\  event\  message ''
is sealed into one record: the server assigns the sequence
number and timestamp (so a writer cannot forge ordering or
backdate an entry) and extends a SHA-256 hash chain over the
record. Reads return empty.
The record is put in one canonical text before it is hashed: every
character that is no visible mark (CR, LF, tab and the other
controls, the Unicode spaces and line separators, the characters
that reorder a line on display) is a blank, runs of blanks are one,
and there is none at either end; the first two words are the source
and the event.
What is sealed is thus what is stored and what a replay reads.
One write is one record: a write of more than 4096 bytes is refused.
The event
.B checkpoint
is the server's own and is refused.
.TP
.B chain
Read-only. The sealed records, oldest first, one per line:
.IR seq \  timestamp \  source \  event \  hash \  message .
.TP
.B head
Read-only. The current chain tip and last sequence number,
.RI `` hash\  seq '',
suitable for publishing or shipping off-host as an anchor.
.TP
.B verify
Read-only. Recomputes the chain from the backing file and returns
.RB `` ok\  \fIn\fP '',
.RB `` broken\ at\ seq\  \fIn\fP ''
(the first record that does not follow from the one before it), or
.RB `` diverged:\  ... ''
(the file is not what the running server wrote: it was changed
behind it).
A file that cannot be read is an error, not a verdict.
.TP
.B pubkey
Read-only. The audit public key (when an audit signer key has been
provisioned in
.IR factotum (4)),
fetched from factotum for distribution to verifiers.
.TP
.B ctl
Write-only. Writing
.B checkpoint
appends a checkpoint record naming the current head. When an audit
signer key is present,
.I auditfs
asks
.IR factotum (4)
to sign it \(em the private key stays in factotum and is never seen
by
.IR auditfs .
The certificate is verifiable with the
.B pubkey
above, giving non-repudiation without sharing any secret.
.PP
Integrity is a linear hash chain:
.IR H [0]
= SHA-256(genesis), and
.IR H [ n ]
= SHA-256(\fIH\fP[\fIn\fP\-1] \(em record). Editing, reordering,
or deleting any record changes that hash and every later one,
so a recomputation that disagrees with the stored chain proves
tampering. The chain is tamper-\fIevident\fP: an externally
anchored
.B head
detects even a full rewrite of the backing file.
.PP
A line of the backing file is a record only if it is exactly the
line the server writes for its fields, and a record follows from the
one before it only if it carries the next sequence number and the
hash of the chain so far.
At startup the chain is recomputed from the backing file and a break
is reported on standard error and in the
.B "auditfs start"
record the server then seals
.RB ( replay=broken@\fIn\fP ).
A file that does not verify is still served and still appended to,
so that writers that must log can, but not as though it verified:
sequence numbers go on from the highest in the file, the chain goes
on from the last record's stored hash, which binds what is added to
the file as it was found,
.B verify
goes on answering
.RB `` broken\ at\ seq\  \fIn\fP '',
and no checkpoint is signed (a write of
.B checkpoint
to
.B ctl
is an error).
To have signed roots again, set the file aside and start a new one.
A backing file that is there and cannot be read stops the server.
.PP
Access control is by namespace placement. Bind only the
.B log
file (write-only) into a subject's name space and it can append
to its own audit trail but cannot read or rewrite history; an
agent thus cannot cover its tracks.
.PP
The backing file defaults to
.BR /usr/inferno/audit/log ;
its containing directory must already exist. With
.B \-f
a different path may be given.
.PP
Auditing is
.B off by default
(most installs do not need it): the boot profile mounts
.I auditfs
only when the opt-in marker
.B /usr/inferno/audit/on
exists. Enable chain-only auditing with
.B touch /usr/inferno/audit/on ;
disable by removing it. Compliance deployments that require AU-2
logging must enable it.
.PP
Checkpoint signing is provided by
.IR factotum (4),
which holds the audit signer key;
.I auditfs
never holds it. Enable auditing and provision signing in one step with
.B sh /lib/sh/audit-setup
(it sets the marker and has factotum generate an ML-DSA-87 key
in-process, sealed into secstore). Until a key is provisioned the
chain is unsigned but still tamper-evident; checkpoints become
signed automatically thereafter.
.PP
.I Auditverify
is the standalone offline verifier. It recomputes the hash chain
over a
.I chainfile
(default standard input) and reports
.RB `` ok ''
or the first break, with the number of breaks and the last sequence
number it read; with
.B \-k
it also checks every checkpoint signature against the audit public
key, and with
.B \-a
that the chain reaches a copy of
.B head
kept elsewhere.
It holds a file to the same rules as the server: each line exactly a
record, sequence numbers 1, 2, 3 and so on, and each checkpoint
naming the head and the sequence number just before it.
The file is read as one stream of bytes; a read error is a failure.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
sh /lib/sh/audit-setup              # enable + factotum mints the signer key
echo 'login unlock user=alice aal=3' >/mnt/audit/log
echo checkpoint >/mnt/audit/ctl
cat /mnt/audit/pubkey >/tmp/auditpub
cat /mnt/audit/chain | auditverify -k /tmp/auditpub
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auditfs.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auditverify.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/auditchain.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/factotum/proto/sign.b
.br
.B /lib/sh/audit-setup
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyring (2),
.IR auditprov (2),
.IR factotum (4),
.IR ventisrv (8)
.br
.B doc/compliance/audit-log-design.md
.br
.B doc/compliance/audit-log-factotum-signing-DESIGN.md
.SH BUGS
Records since the last signed
.B ctl
checkpoint are protected by the hash chain but are not yet covered
by a signature; checkpoint frequently (and on high-value events) to
bound that window.
.PP
High-volume agent-provenance payloads are no longer inlined: they
are content-addressed into a
.IR ventisrv (8)
store via
.IR auditprov (2),
with the SHA-256 pin sealed under the chain.
